Disadvantages of .md files over .json

Here are the real disadvantages of .md (Markdown) compared to .json when designing agent systems—explained from a practical, system-design perspective:

⚠️ Disadvantages of .md over .json
1. ❌ No Execution Control
Markdown cannot define:

Parallel vs sequential execution

Timeouts

Retries

Dependencies between agents

👉 Example:

.md: “Run agents in parallel” (just text)

.json: "strategy": "parallel" (actually enforceable)

2. ❌ Not Machine-Friendly
Hard for programs to parse reliably

No strict schema or validation

Prone to inconsistent formatting

👉 JSON:

Structured

Schema-validatable

Easily parsed by any runtime

3. ❌ No Native Orchestration
You cannot define:

Agent pipelines

Fan-out / fan-in

Aggregation logic

👉 .json can:

{
  "strategy": "parallel",
  "aggregation": "merge"
}
4. ❌ Difficult to Scale
As system grows:

.md becomes long and messy

Hard to track multiple agents

No modular structure

👉 JSON:

Supports nesting

Easy to organize multiple agents

5. ❌ No Type Safety / Validation
Markdown:

Free-form text

No guarantees

JSON:

Can enforce:

Required fields

Data types

Structure

6. ❌ Weak Tooling Support
Limited IDE validation

No autocomplete for structure

👉 JSON:

Strong IDE support (IntelliJ, VS Code)

Schema validation

Linting

7. ❌ Hard to Integrate with Systems
Markdown is not ideal for:

APIs

Backend services

Automation pipelines

👉 JSON is the standard for:

REST APIs

Config-driven systems

Agent frameworks

8. ❌ Cannot Represent Dynamic Behavior
You can’t express:

Conditional execution

Priority handling

Conflict resolution

👉 JSON:

{
  "priority": ["security", "npe"],
  "conflictResolution": "severity-based"
}
9. ❌ Poor for Parallel Systems (Critical for You)
Since you’re building multi-agent systems:

👉 .md:

Cannot actually run agents in parallel

👉 .json:

Designed for orchestration + concurrency

